WEBVTT

00:00:01.630 --> 00:00:06.923
Hi, I'm Mengwei, a PhD candidate at the
University of Barcelona and the Barcelona

00:00:06.963 --> 00:00:08.517
Institute of Economics.

00:00:08.852 --> 00:00:11.480
My job market paper is
called Museums Around:

00:00:11.556 --> 00:00:14.498
Cultural Infrastructure
and Educational Outcomes.

00:00:14.759 --> 00:00:19.664
In this paper, I find that a museum opening
near home raises students' exam results,

00:00:19.704 --> 00:00:22.165
and shapes what they choose to study.

00:00:22.926 --> 00:00:27.275
Societies build cultural institutions
to keep what is known and valued,

00:00:27.315 --> 00:00:28.591
and to pass it on.

00:00:28.759 --> 00:00:32.257
There are more than 100,000
museums around the world nowadays.

00:00:32.704 --> 00:00:36.498
Once built, they become part of
the environment where people live,

00:00:36.574 --> 00:00:40.313
and for the young they may shape what
they achieve and what they study.

00:00:40.796 --> 00:00:44.460
But, because of data scarcity
and identification difficulties,

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:49.072
whether museums do affect the young
people around them is mostly unknown.

00:00:49.741 --> 00:00:52.480
Conceptually, they may, for two reasons.

00:00:53.111 --> 00:00:57.145
First, museums present knowledge
through original objects,

00:00:57.185 --> 00:01:01.757
which may stimulate curiosity and
improve academic performance in general.

00:01:02.167 --> 00:01:05.312
And second, they are
organised around themes,

00:01:05.352 --> 00:01:08.534
so they may also steer students
towards related subjects,

00:01:08.574 --> 00:01:14.072
a choice students often make with little
information about what a field involves.

00:01:14.377 --> 00:01:20.077
In this paper, I use admin data on
8.5 million students in England,

00:01:20.117 --> 00:01:23.264
and link it to a census of UK museums.

00:01:23.728 --> 00:01:26.114
With a stacked difference-in-differences,

00:01:26.154 --> 00:01:30.262
I compare students whose neighbourhood
gets a new museum with students whose

00:01:30.302 --> 00:01:32.466
neighbourhood gets one
several years later,

00:01:32.506 --> 00:01:37.356
to examine whether museums around can affect
students' upper-secondary performance,

00:01:37.506 --> 00:01:39.727
progression, and subject choices.

00:01:40.265 --> 00:01:42.171
Let's look at attainment effects.

00:01:42.654 --> 00:01:46.227
Before the museum opens, the
two groups move in parallel.

00:01:46.525 --> 00:01:52.300
After it opens, students move up in
the upper-secondary exam ranking by 1.5

00:01:52.340 --> 00:01:53.597
percentile points.

00:01:53.821 --> 00:01:56.170
That means, in a class of 100 students,

00:01:56.210 --> 00:01:58.412
you move up by one and a half places.

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:00.874
The effect fades with distance,

00:02:00.914 --> 00:02:04.503
from about 1.5 points within 1 and 2 km,

00:02:04.543 --> 00:02:07.467
to nearly zero by 10 km.

00:02:07.914 --> 00:02:13.208
The gain is about twice as large for
students from disadvantaged backgrounds.

00:02:13.858 --> 00:02:18.485
On average, progression to upper-secondary
education is largely unchanged.

00:02:18.525 --> 00:02:23.225
But students in the middle of the
prior-attainment distribution become about 1

00:02:23.265 --> 00:02:25.745
percentage point more likely to progress.

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:29.533
Museums also shape what
students choose to study.

00:02:29.898 --> 00:02:32.697
I pair each museum theme
with subject areas,

00:02:32.737 --> 00:02:34.962
and estimate the treatment effects.

00:02:35.433 --> 00:02:38.623
On the diagonal of this effect matrix,

00:02:38.701 --> 00:02:41.515
you see that most themes
raise their own subjects.

00:02:41.862 --> 00:02:47.694
Science and nature museums raise academic
STEM entry by 2.5 percentage points,

00:02:47.790 --> 00:02:51.248
and industry museums raise
STEM entry by about 1.

00:02:51.398 --> 00:02:55.947
Social-life museums raise
the social sciences by 1.2,

00:02:55.987 --> 00:03:00.087
and history museums raise
humanities entries by 0.9.

00:03:00.433 --> 00:03:03.518
Arts museums do not
change entry into arts,

00:03:03.558 --> 00:03:08.286
possibly because students already
encountered the arts in many other ways.

00:03:08.326 --> 00:03:13.765
Off the diagonal, the effects are
mostly small and not significant.

00:03:14.148 --> 00:03:15.873
The exception is science museums,

00:03:16.005 --> 00:03:20.498
which significantly move students from
other subjects towards academic STEM.

00:03:20.898 --> 00:03:23.051
With only a few science openings,

00:03:23.273 --> 00:03:26.051
I do not want to go too
far interpreting this.

00:03:26.808 --> 00:03:29.569
The paper speaks to three
strands of literature.

00:03:29.630 --> 00:03:33.715
First, on how the adolescent environment
shapes young people's outcomes.

00:03:33.755 --> 00:03:37.908
We already know that moving to
a better neighbourhood helps,

00:03:38.148 --> 00:03:40.462
but it changes the whole bundle at once.

00:03:40.665 --> 00:03:44.286
Recent works look at
specific local inputs,

00:03:44.326 --> 00:03:46.265
such as new schools, broadband,

00:03:46.433 --> 00:03:48.105
youth clubs and libraries.

00:03:48.469 --> 00:03:53.161
I provide population-scale causal evidence
that a cultural input in the environment

00:03:53.201 --> 00:03:55.551
affects educational outcomes.

00:03:55.790 --> 00:03:59.983
Second, on subject choices.
College majors are well studied,

00:04:00.023 --> 00:04:04.408
and they are partially rooted
in secondary education choices.

00:04:04.773 --> 00:04:08.837
Secondary education choices are
shown to be shaped by schools,

00:04:08.933 --> 00:04:12.569
families and role models,
and most work is on STEM.

00:04:13.058 --> 00:04:16.840
I link local environment to
what students choose to study,

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:18.890
across all subject groups.

00:04:19.344 --> 00:04:21.822
Third, on place-based policies.

00:04:21.862 --> 00:04:26.533
Investments in culture are
often judged by local economies:

00:04:27.148 --> 00:04:30.375
the housing market, economic
development and labour market.

00:04:30.415 --> 00:04:35.587
I evaluate cultural infrastructure
by its effects on young people,

00:04:35.683 --> 00:04:37.765
the next generation of the society.

00:04:38.470 --> 00:04:44.208
So, to conclude, the paper shows that
museums nearby shape how well students

00:04:44.248 --> 00:04:46.894
perform, and what they choose to study.

00:04:47.137 --> 00:04:48.007
Thank you very much.
